From c52247312a9f7bfa66f09e3f93d7a5b1ede86359 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: J P <jpwatters@mac.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 16:18:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/35] mac-serial-bridge: the NAS-shared QMX as a serial port
 on a Mac

tools/mac-serial-bridge/qmx_serial_bridge.py creates a pseudo-terminal at
~/cu.QMX-NAS and bridges it to the relay's QMX serial port 3 (TCP 7356), so
WSJT-X, JTDX, fldigi, rigctld or a logger can use the radio over the network
as if it were on the Mac's USB. CAT only, no audio. Python 3 standard library.

It finds the NAS like the Tab5's Find NAS: broadcasts QMXR? on UDP 7355 and
takes the address from "QMXR! <ip> <port>" (the advertised port is the QMXR/1
service, so port 3 is used on that address). Searches at start-up and when
the remembered address stops answering; --host fixes it, --fallback-host is
used when nobody answers, --discover lists relays.

Connects only while a program uses the port and releases port 3 after 120 s
idle; keeps echo off, flushes stale replies, and explains a refused port 3.
install-mac.sh makes it a login item (LaunchAgent); uninstall-mac.sh removes it.

QMX-Panadapter-Shared.pdf: chapters 13-16 (bridge, sources, driver listing,
application listings) added; SharedNAS-USB-Readme.pdf is the bridge readme.

Tested on Linux against qmx_nas_relay.py --fake (discovery, probe, nettest
PASSED) and stand-ins (idle release, relay down, port 3 refused, echo, pyserial,
junk/duplicate/multiple answers, NAS changing address). Not yet run on macOS
or against the real NAS.

